p3=f'''<p><span class="b">What each agent decides and remembers.</span> Each year t, a household or firm n chooses a vehicle, how often to cross and when, from a choice model where the instruments in force I(t) set the cost. Acceptance is estimated in its core, cross-sectional form in A1, as an integrated choice and latent-variable (ICLV) model on real wave-1 data, the hybrid-choice approach used to read acceptance surveys elsewhere [hybridaccept], extending Le Pira, Marcucci and Gatta from negotiating stakeholder groups to a population of individually estimated agents [lepira]. Acceptance depends on the dose actually paid, the information arm and covariates. Whether that latent construct is cleanly identified, and whether it persists, is the thesis’s first empirical test; the pathway machinery is built on its answer.</p>
<div class="eq">(1)&nbsp;&nbsp;U(n,j,t) = β·cost(j, I(t)) + θ′·x(n,j) + ε(n,j,t)<br>(2)&nbsp;&nbsp;A*(n,t) = ρ·A(n,t−1) + δ·dose(n,t) + η·info(n) + γ′·z(n) + u(n,t)<br>(3)&nbsp;&nbsp;pathway p is admissible if the share with A* ≥ λ holds every year; its acceptance margin is the largest rise in λ for which p stays admissible.</div>
<p>The dynamic form (2) adds one first-order persistence term, identified between the two waves with the initial-conditions problem handled [wooldridge]. This ρ is a Level-3 extension, pursued only if both waves land; the core contribution does not rest on it. I claim only what the data identify: a cross-sectional acceptance model as the floor, one persistence term as the stretch, never a full dynamic panel.</p>
<div class="plate"><span class="platelabel">What memory does</span>{FIG3}<div class="figcap">Three agents over 2026–2050: the commuter crosses λ in 2031 and holds; the occasional crosser never leaves; the van driver, with the largest dose, falls below λ from 2035. <span class="tier">ILLUSTRATIVE · ρ and dose shown are not estimates</span></div></div>
<p><span class="b">What a pathway is, and how it is chosen.</span> Five instruments: revision of the package, the zero-emission rate, a passenger distance charge, a heavy-vehicle distance charge, and earmarking of revenue. A3 is a search, not a catalogue: choose the switch-on year and level of each instrument to maximise the share of futures meeting all three 2050 targets and minimise maximum regret, subject to acceptance above threshold every year, revenue covering debt service, and an accessibility floor, with a many-objective robust algorithm over the ensemble and scenario discovery naming where the robust set still breaks [prim, mordm]. Treating robustness as the quantity to optimise follows the stability-metrics line of Shaabani, Hvattum, Hoff and Laporte [shaabani]. Full enumeration of a reduced set of four of the five instruments (ordered switch-on sequences, each later switch at one of three thresholds) is 4 + 36 + 216 + 648 = 904, kept to check the search; the full search ranges over switch-years and levels for all five, on the order of 10⁴ pathways.</p>
<table class="ttable"><tr><th>Target in 2050</th><th>Metric</th><th>Anchor</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Emissions</td><td>Well-to-wheel CO₂e from road traffic in the case area</td><td>Climate Change Act: 90–95% by 2050 [klimalov]</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Finance</td><td>Toll revenue ≥ debt service; failure if below three years running</td><td>Package financing [innst337]</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Accessibility</td><td>Generalised cost of the lowest income quintile no higher than 2026</td><td>Distributional floor, a design choice</td></tr></table>
<p><span class="b">What would falsify it.</span> If ρ is near zero, agents need no memory and claim 1 reduces to a static result; if η is near zero, information does not move acceptance and H2 fails; if the fast engine cannot reproduce the network model within tolerance, the ensemble is cut to what the network model can run. Each is reported whichever way it falls.</p>
<p><span class="b">Tools, and the honest skill path.</span> Python throughout: Biogeme for estimation, the EMA Workbench for the ensemble and scenario discovery, OSMnx and NetworkX for networks, OR-Tools for routing checks, and NGM elasticities for the freight chains. My foundation is a logistics master from this faculty and an operations-research and programming background; I have not estimated choice models professionally, and I do not claim to. As a reproducibility check I estimated a standard mixed logit on the public Swissmetro data in Biogeme (N = 6,768, 1,000 Halton draws), reproducing the published normal-mixture reference (final log-likelihood −5214.9 against about −5214.8) [evdcm]. Choice-model estimation is the first named skill I deepen, in Year-1 coursework and under supervision in the Transport Research Group (TRG) at Molde University College.</p>
<div class="handoff">→ Tested where acceptance is live and freight is real.</div>
<div class="foot"><span>Arshad Akhtar Abbasia</span><span>The model</span><span class="r">github.com/Astheno-sphere/computational_logistics · 3 / 6</span></div>'''
page3=f'<section class="sheet"><div class="secnum">03 · THE MODEL</div><h2>What each agent <span class="g">decides and remembers</span></h2>{p3}</section>'

p4=f'''<p><span class="b">Why Kristiansund, and where freight enters.</span> Bypakke Kristiansund began collection on 1 June 2026 at two rv70 stations, Nordsundbrua and Omsundbrua, in both directions; about 70% of the package is toll-financed over up to 15 years [bypakke, innst337]. A car pays NOK 34, 27.20 with a tag, 19.04 if zero-emission with a tag [vegamot]. The council approved the basis 26 to 19 and the county 33 to 14: acceptance here is contested and live. The town sits on the Møre og Romsdal west-coast corridor, where the Ferjefri E39 programme and NGM both treat coastal short-sea shipping as the competing mode for freight bound to Oslo and the continent [ngm]. Freight is estimated here, not borrowed: a short carrier stated-choice block gives the mode-and-timing response of hauliers to a distance charge, which the NGM chains carry to the network. Because the heavy fleet turns over slowly and the sea alternative needs volume to stay viable, a pathway that reaches road freight too late closes the modal-shift window and locks the emissions in. The thesis reports the year the freight signpost must fire, estimated from carrier behaviour; it is expected to fire before the passenger one, an expectation the design tests rather than a result it assumes. Molde, with no package, is the passenger comparison town.</p>
<div class="plate"><span class="platelabel teal">Freight · acceptance live</span>
<div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden;margin-top:8px"><img src="{MAP}" style="width:100%;display:block"></div>
<div class="figcap">The toll is not one price; it is a map of who can avoid it. Every building is assigned to the side of Nordsundbrua it reaches without the bridge (OSM graph). Red: Nordlandet, which only the bridge connects. Teal: the toll-free Atlanterhavstunnelen. Trip 1 pays at a station; trips 2 and 3 do not. <span class="tier">REAL · OSM graph</span></div></div>
<div class="twocol"><div><h3>Survey — three blocks, Bayesian D-efficient</h3>
<p class="small"><b>Acceptance block</b> (households): information randomised in three arms — personal cost as numbers, purpose as a short narrative, and control; the arms test format as well as content. <b>Passenger stated-choice block:</b> vehicle at next replacement, crossing frequency and time, under tariff and discount levels inside national ranges. <b>Carrier stated-choice block</b> (firms and hauliers): mode (road vs coastal short-sea), route and timing under a distance charge, single wave, small targeted sample — the estimated freight element, NGM-anchored. Kristiansund carries household and carrier design; Molde the acceptance block. Wave 1 and the carrier block in month 6; wave 2 in month 18 with the same households. Planning sample 1,428 households in Kristiansund, 714 in Molde, sized by minimum detectable effect.</p></div>
<div><h3>Hypotheses</h3><p class="small"><b>H1</b> acceptance persists once a charge is permanent (ρ &gt; 0), tested as within-person change between waves against dose. <b>H2</b> information moves acceptance independently of experience (η ≠ 0), tested by the randomised arms, turning the Norwegian correlation between information and attitude [odeck08] into a causal estimate. <b>H3 (freight)</b> a distance charge shifts carrier mode choice toward short-sea above a threshold rate, estimated from the carrier block.</p>
<h3>Freight fallback</h3><p class="small">If the carrier sample under-powers, the freight arm reverts to NGM mode-split elasticities with a reported sensitivity on the key elasticity; the freight signpost is then a modelled consequence, and the thesis ladder absorbs it without loss of a complete dissertation.</p></div></div>
<div class="handoff">→ And here is the three-year plan to deliver it.</div>
<div class="foot"><span>Arshad Akhtar Abbasia</span><span>Case and design</span><span class="r">github.com/Astheno-sphere/computational_logistics · 4 / 6</span></div>'''
page4=f'<section class="sheet"><div class="secnum">04 · CASE AND DESIGN</div><h2>Kristiansund, where <span class="g">acceptance is live</span></h2>{p4}</section>'

tools='''<div class="tools">
<div class="tcol"><div class="tcat">Estimate</div><div class="ttool">Biogeme</div><div class="ttool s">mixed logit · ICLV</div><div class="trole">Acceptance and carrier mode choice estimated on real survey data, not assumed.</div><div class="tart">A1 · A2</div></div>
<div class="tcol"><div class="tcat">Explore</div><div class="ttool">EMA Workbench</div><div class="ttool s">ε-NSGA-II · PRIM</div><div class="trole">1,000 futures, robust search over pathways, scenario discovery where the robust set breaks (MORDM).</div><div class="tart">A3</div></div>
<div class="tcol"><div class="tcat">Network</div><div class="ttool">OSMnx · NetworkX</div><div class="ttool s">OR-Tools · MATSim</div><div class="trole">Real road graphs, routing checks, and a dynamic cross-check the fast engine is measured against.</div><div class="tart">A1 · A3</div></div>
<div class="tcol"><div class="tcat">Freight</div><div class="ttool">NGM</div><div class="ttool s">national freight model</div><div class="trole">Commodity chains, mode split, class load factors and empty running carried to the network.</div><div class="tart">A3</div></div>
<div class="tcol"><div class="tcat">Compose</div><div class="ttool">Rhino · Grasshopper</div><div class="ttool s">Ladybug · Blender</div><div class="trole">Every figure computed from the data, geo-referenced and drawn in one parametric pipeline.</div><div class="tart">all plates</div></div></div>'''
p5=f'''<p><span class="b">The ensemble and the search, priced.</span> The robust search explores on the order of 10⁴ candidate pathways, each scored against a Latin-hypercube sample of futures, with the surviving robust set re-checked on the full 10³ futures. Each evaluation advances a zonal state to 2050, about 25 ms, so the budget is roughly 40 to 150 CPU-hours, embarrassingly parallel and inside a desktop over a weekend. The fast engine’s fidelity is measured, not assumed: on the real OpenStreetMap graphs of Molde and Kristiansund it reproduces static-network car travel times per zone pair within about 15% mean absolute error (r 0.73 to 0.84) at three to four orders of magnitude less compute [evfid]. A dynamic cross-check against MATSim (Molde, 4,000 agents, 20 iterations) confirms the regime: at free flow the fast engine matches MATSim to about 19% (r 0.74) and the static assignment to 6% (r 0.999); under a tenfold capacity squeeze the free-flow engine breaks down, as such engines must, which is exactly why robust pathways are re-checked on the network model [evfid]. Passenger and freight vehicles carry class-specific load factors and empty running from NGM, which is what makes this a logistics question as well as a passenger one.</p>
<div class="rfwrap" style="margin:6px 0 14px">{RF}</div>
<div class="twocol"><div class="plate"><span class="platelabel">What replaces the lost revenue</span>{FIG5B}</div>
<div><h3 style="margin-top:0">The three articles</h3><table class="ttable"><tr><th>Article</th><th>Submit</th><th>Data &amp; fieldwork</th></tr>
<tr><td>A1 Choice estimated: household acceptance + carrier mode choice</td><td>M14</td><td>Wave 1 + carrier block; recovery and fidelity tests</td></tr>
<tr><td>A2 Acceptance that persists</td><td>M26</td><td>Both household waves</td></tr>
<tr><td>A3 Robust passenger and freight pathways</td><td>M34</td><td>Ensemble; estimated carrier choice (NGM fallback); dated events</td></tr></table></div></div>
<h3>The computational stack — one tool named per article</h3>{tools}
<div class="twocol"><div><h3>A thesis that survives a failed survey</h3>
<p class="small"><b>Level 1</b>, no new data: A1 on national ranges and A3 on NGM elasticities, freight included, are a thesis. <b>Level 2</b>, wave 1 + carrier block: adds H2, a cross-sectional A2, estimated freight (H3). <b>Level 3</b>, both waves: adds H1 and the dynamic persistence term, the stretch. Each level is a complete dissertation; the survey raises its ceiling. The guaranteed spine is my own collected survey, under my control.</p>
<h3>Calendar</h3><p class="small">SSB and RVU microdata applications filed in week 1, off the critical path. Months 1–3: coursework incl. philosophy of science; Sikt notification; pilot with Norwegian-speaking co-authors. Month 6 wave 1 + carrier block · M14 A1 · M18 wave 2 · M26 A2 · M34 A3 · M34–36 summary. Articles are pipelined, not serial.</p></div>
<div><h3>Risk and response</h3><table class="ttable"><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Response</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Low response (wave 1)</td><td>Commercial panel top-up; Level 2 holds</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Attrition (wave 2)</td><td>Sample sized for it; H1 reported with its detectable effect</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">No microdata by month 6</td><td>Published ranges + NGM elasticities; spine is the own survey</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Carrier sample under-powers</td><td>Revert freight to NGM elasticities with reported sensitivity</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Engine diverges</td><td>Larger network subset, fewer pathways</td></tr></table></div></div>
<p class="small"><span class="b">Why here.</span> TRANSPLAN’s backcasting and its call for realistic, goal-directed plans [toi2172]; Molde’s strength in stated choice and transport economics and the DCM and ABM tradition in the TRG; and a Logistics programme where freight vehicle classes belong. Partners as possibilities: TØI for the national experiment’s ranges and NGM, CICERO for climate-policy acceptance, the Norwegian Computing Center for surrogate methods. <span class="b">Receipts.</span> Every number traces to a tagged commit, with 89 automated tests, and figures are generated from the same code; code released openly, anonymised survey data deposited FAIR once Sikt allows; AI-tool use disclosed in the supplement. <span class="b">Dissemination.</span> Three journal articles (A1–A3), a Norwegian-language piece in <i>Samferdsel</i> in year 3, and presentation at the annual NORS conference, where I have presented before.</p>
<div class="handoff">→ The signpost that warns a path is failing is the same erosion page 1 showed already under way. The ring closes.</div>
<div class="foot"><span>Arshad Akhtar Abbasia</span><span>Plan, robustness and receipts</span><span class="r">github.com/Astheno-sphere/computational_logistics · 5 / 6</span></div>'''
page5=f'<section class="sheet"><div class="secnum">05 · PLAN, ROBUSTNESS AND RECEIPTS</div><h2>The robust front, and <span class="g">how it finishes in three years</span></h2>{p5}</section>'

